-- title:
--   Theorem 5.9 — Algorithm 1 is correct and runs in polynomial time
-- statement:
--   Consider a game $G$ with a reward function $r$, a succinct discount factor $\lambda=1-2^{-2^{B_\lambda}}$, additive error $\varepsilon=2^{-B_\varepsilon}$, and a tuple $(\sigma,\tau,I_\sigma,I_\tau)$ of power-series numbers. Then
--   1. (Correctness) Algorithm 1 returns true if and only if the tuple is a witness;
--   2. (Running time) Algorithm 1 runs in time polynomial in the size of the input.
--
--   In item 2 the output of Algorithm 1 is computed by a polynomial-time Turing machine from the binary encoding of the game, the reward function, $\lambda$, $\varepsilon$ and the tuple.
--
--   The verifier is what places the witness search problem in TFNP.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: a finite game G = (S, A, B, δ) with |S| = n and at most m actions per player → states Fin n, actions Fin mA and Fin mB with n, mA, mB ≥ 1, m = max(mA, mB), distributions as nonnegative real vectors summing to one; reason: relabelling of finite sets; nonempty state and action sets are the source game convention
--   - encoding: the probability measure on valid plays Ω_s generated by finite histories, strategies on valid histories → the Ionescu-Tulcea trajectory measure on all sequences in (S × A × B)^ℕ; strategies are defined on all finite stage lists; reason: invalid plays have probability zero and values of strategies on invalid histories never matter, so every expectation and value is unchanged
--   - encoding: input: rational probabilities and rewards; ε = 2^{-Bε} given in binary with Bε + 1 bits; a succinct discount factor λ = 1 − 2^{-2^{Bλ}} → self-delimiting binary codes of n, |A|, |B| and all rationals; ε encoded as the binary natural 2^{Bε}; λ encoded as the binary natural 2^{Bλ}; a state as a binary natural; a target set as its characteristic vector; Bε ≥ 1 in valid instances; reason: these are binary encodings of exactly the source's inputs, with lengths linear in Bε and Bλ as the source specifies; malformed strings are not valid instances (outside the problem)
--   - encoding: "Algorithm 1 runs in time polynomial in the size of the input" → Algorithm 1 is the Lean Boolean function algorithm1 (lines 2–13 with the shrinking checks in the expanded form of Equation (1)); some Cook machine computes [algorithm1 I W] from the encoding of (I, W) in polynomial time for every valid instance I; reason: the step count of a pseudocode algorithm is expressed through a machine computing the same input-output map; the machine is required on every valid input
-- source:
--   Ali Asadi, Krishnendu Chatterjee, The Computational Complexity of Concurrent Stochastic Games: Exact Value and Approximation, STOC 2027 submission #285 (October 2026), SHA-256 5cff187cf6b230b3bdfe540f2832f824d1ddb6acb43239fbf469d37c43b0d461, https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-1/285.pdf, Theorem 5.9 and Algorithm 1, printed p. 16 (PDF p. 18)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ConcurrentStochasticApprox_Approx_Game
import Definitions.Def_ConcurrentStochasticApprox_Approx_PowerSeries
import Definitions.Def_ConcurrentStochasticApprox_Approx_Problems
open Finset

namespace ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx

/-- **Theorem 5.9 (Polynomial-time verifier).** For a game `G` with reward `r`, succinct discount
factor `λ` and additive error `ε = 2^{-Bε}`, and a tuple `(σ, τ, I_σ, I_τ)`:
1. (Correctness) Algorithm 1 returns true if and only if the tuple is a witness;
2. (Running time) Algorithm 1 runs in time polynomial in the size of the input: a Turing machine
   computes its output from the binary encoding of the input in polynomial time. -/
theorem theorem_5_9 :
    (∀ (I : DiscInst) (hI : I.Valid) (W : WitnessData I.G.n I.G.mA I.G.mB),
      algorithm1 I W = true ↔ IsWitness (I.G.toCSG hI.1) I.r.toReal I.Blam I.Bε W) ∧
    ∃ f : List Bool → List Bool, CookPvsNP.PolyTimeComputable f ∧
      ∀ (I : DiscInst) (W : WitnessData I.G.n I.G.mA I.G.mB), I.Valid →
        f (I.enc ++ W.enc) = [algorithm1 I W] := by sorry

end ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx
